(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_chemistry_hydrocarbons=[{id:"neet-2026-q061",pn:61,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Hydrocarbons",st:"Stereochemistry of addition to alkenes",q:`Given below are two statements :
Statement I : trans-But-2-ene upon treatment with Br\u2082 in CCl\u2084 gives the following product.
Statement II : cis-But-2-ene upon treatment with alkaline KMnO\u2084 gives the following product.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],c:2,s:`Statement I: trans-but-2-ene with Br\u2082/CCl\u2084 gives anti addition. The product drawn in the Newman projection converts to a Fischer projection that is not meso.
The meso product comes from cis-but-2-ene, not from trans. So Statement I is incorrect.
Statement II: cis-but-2-ene with alkaline KMnO\u2084 gives syn addition of two OH groups.
The Newman projection given converts to the meso-butane-2,3-diol. So Statement II is correct.`},{id:"neet-2026-q082",pn:82,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Hydrocarbons",st:"Conjugated dienes",q:"Among the following, the compound having conjugated double bonds is",o:["hepta-1,4-diene","hepta-1,5-diene","hepta-1,6-diene","hepta-1,3-diene"],c:3,s:`A conjugated diene has two double bonds separated by one single bond.
hepta-1,3-diene: CH\u2083\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH=CH\u2013CH=CH\u2082, double bonds at C1 and C3, one single bond between them.
hepta-1,4-diene: CH\u2083\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH=CH\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH=CH\u2082 has C1=C2 and C4=C5 with a CH\u2082 between them (not conjugated).
hepta-1,5-diene: CH\u2083\u2013CH=CH\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH=CH\u2082 is not conjugated.
hepta-1,6-diene: H\u2082C=CH\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH=CH\u2082 is not conjugated.
Note: Publisher's printed chain for hepta-1,4-diene looks garbled; I wrote the correct structures.`},{id:"neet-2026-q087",pn:87,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Hydrocarbons",st:"Alkyne reduction and HBr addition",q:`Consider the following reaction sequences and choose the correct option.
Ph\u2013C\u2261C\u2013Me on treatment with H\u2082, Pd/C (Lindlar's catalyst) gives K; K on treatment with HBr gives M.
Ph\u2013C\u2261C\u2013Me on treatment with Na/liq. NH\u2083 gives L; L on treatment with HBr and benzoyl peroxide gives N.`,o:["K and L are enantiomers","M and N are geometrical isomers","M and N are stereoisomers","K and L are geometrical isomers"],c:3,s:`Lindlar's catalyst gives the cis alkene K (Ph and Me on the same side).
Na/liq. NH\u2083 gives the trans alkene L (Ph and CH\u2083 on opposite sides).
K and L differ only in the arrangement about the double bond, so they are geometrical isomers.
HBr adds to K to give M = Ph\u2013CH(Br)\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH\u2083 (Br on the carbon next to Ph).
HBr with benzoyl peroxide adds to L in the anti-Markovnikov way to give N = Ph\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH(Br)\u2013CH\u2083.`}]})();
